Yes, that works. The repo is just Python test files, nothing compiled, so where you clone it doesn't matter.

On your Windows PC:

```powershell
cd $env:USERPROFILE
git clone https://gitcode.com/TritonAscendTest/Q3TritonKernel.git
```

It will prompt for `yaolu0211` and your personal access token.

Then copy it up. Your SSH config already has `a5` with ProxyJump through fremont, and scp uses the same config:

```powershell
scp -r $env:USERPROFILE\Q3TritonKernel a5:/home/yaolu/
```

On the A5:

```bash
source /home/yaolu/env.sh
cd /home/yaolu/Q3TritonKernel
ls
pytest -s tests/fla_perf/test_nsa_compression_kernels_perf.py::test_nsa_compression_fwd_kernel_perf
```

One thing to check first. If the repo has Python dependencies you'll need them installed in `louis_env`, and the A5 can't reach PyPI any more than it could reach gitcode. Look at what it wants before running:

```bash
cat requirements.txt 2>/dev/null
```

If there's a list and packages are missing, that's the same connectivity problem again and worth raising with Hocky rather than working around. The conda env you cloned from `chaoran_cv` may already have everything, since it was presumably set up for exactly this work.
